  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 319 AM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nSlightly cooler, wetter conditions continue Saturday with a 50 to 80 \npercent chance of showers and thunderstorms during the afternoon and \nevening. Bertha will be a less of a moisture contributor by this \njuncture, but convective chances/coverage look to be enhanced by a \nshortwave trough pushing SE across the state during the afternoon \nand evening timed with peak diurnal heating. Localized flash \nflooding will continue to be a primary concern with model forecast \nsoundings maintaining PWATs over 2 inches and relatively weak \nsteering flow, with this risk locally enhanced in locations that \nbecome saturated from a previous day's storms. WPC maintains a \nMarginal Risk of excessive rainfall across the entire CWA into \nSaturday.\n\nMore of a northwest flow regime still looks to take over starting \nSunday and continuing into the first half of next week, as a strong, \nbroad ridge of nearly stationary high pressure builds across the \nwestern and central CONUS. Increased mid-level dry air and lower \nPWATs will allow Georgia to trend back into a more typical summer \ndiurnal convection pattern with much more isolated coverage of \nstorms each afternoon, pending any disturbance in the NW flow riding \ndown the east side of the ridge to focus/increase chances of \norganized convection. Hints remain in long-range model guidance of \nsomething trying to ride this flow Tuesday evening into Wednesday \nwhich could mean some sort of MCS, but overall forecast confidence \nremains low at this range. The heat and humidity also look to return \nin earnest from Monday onward, with high temperatures rising back \ninto the mid to upper 90s and heat index values reaching 100 to 110. \nA modest response is now seen in the ECMWF Extreme Forecast Index \n(EFI) by Tuesday and Wednesday for high temperatures (+0.5 to 0.7), \nsuggesting a somewhat unusual heat event based on a 20-year model \nclimatology focused on a 5-week period representing this time of \nyear climatologically. Days with a lack of synoptic support for \nincreased thunderstorm coverage (and thus less cloud cover) will see \nthe highest temperatures and heat index values. Bottom line...we'll \nbe watching both thunderstorm chances and the heat forecast for \npotential hazards next week. 500mb height ensemble cluster analysis \ncurrently suggests this overall synoptic pattern isn't likely to \nchange even into the second half of next week.",
